const faqItems = [
  { question: "What is Michigan's no-fault insurance system?", answer: "Michigan's no-fault insurance system requires auto insurance policies to include Personal Injury Protection (PIP) benefits that cover your medical expenses, lost wages, and replacement services regardless of who caused the accident. The system was designed to ensure injured victims receive prompt medical care and wage replacement without having to prove fault. However, the law has been significantly amended in recent years, and the benefits available depend on when you purchased your policy and what coverage level you chose." },
  { question: "What PIP benefits am I entitled to after a car accident in Ann Arbor?", answer: "PIP benefits typically include: (1) medical expenses for reasonable and necessary treatment related to your injuries, (2) wage loss benefits up to 85% of your gross income for up to three years, (3) replacement services for household tasks you can't perform due to injuries, (4) attendant care or nursing services if needed, (5) rehabilitation costs, and (6) mileage reimbursement for medical appointments. The exact amount of coverage depends on your policy's PIP limits—older policies may have unlimited coverage while newer policies have chosen coverage levels." },
  { question: "How do I file a no-fault PIP claim after an accident in Ann Arbor?", answer: "You should notify your auto insurance company as soon as possible after the accident—ideally within 24 to 48 hours. Most policies require written notice within 30 days. Provide basic information about the accident, your injuries, and treating providers. You'll need to complete an application for benefits and submit medical records supporting your claims. If your insurance company denies benefits or delays payment, you can file a lawsuit. Michigan's one-year back rule allows you to sue for unpaid benefits, but prompt action strengthens your case." },
  { question: "What if my insurance company denies my PIP claim?", answer: "Insurance companies deny PIP claims for many reasons, often improperly. Common denial tactics include arguing your treatment wasn't reasonable or necessary, claiming you failed to attend independent medical examinations, asserting your injuries aren't related to the accident, or citing late notice. You have the right to challenge these denials. Our attorneys file lawsuits against insurance companies that improperly deny or terminate PIP benefits, and we can recover benefits plus attorney fees when denials are unreasonable." },
  { question: "Can I choose my own doctor for no-fault PIP treatment in Michigan?", answer: "Yes. Under Michigan's no-fault law, you have the right to choose your treating physician. Your insurance company may request that you attend an independent medical examination (IME) with a doctor of their choosing, but you are not required to switch your primary treatment to that doctor. However, your insurance may argue that your treatment isn't reasonable or necessary if you don't follow their recommended providers. We can help you navigate these disputes and protect your right to appropriate care." },
  { question: "What is the serious impairment threshold and why does it matter?", answer: "The serious impairment of body function threshold is a legal standard that determines when you can sue the at-fault driver for pain and suffering damages. Under Michigan law, you must prove that your injuries caused serious impairment of an important body function, or that you suffered permanent serious disfigurement. This standard is one of the most contested issues in Michigan personal injury cases. Insurance companies fight hard to keep cases below this threshold. Our attorneys build strong evidence to meet the serious impairment standard and pursue full compensation." },
  { question: "How long do I have to file a no-fault insurance lawsuit in Michigan?", answer: "For PIP benefits, Michigan law gives you one year from the date the benefit was incurred to file a lawsuit. For example, if your insurance company denied payment for medical treatment provided six months after the accident, you have one year from the date of that treatment to sue. For personal injury (third-party) claims against the at-fault driver, the statute of limitations is three years. Acting quickly is essential because evidence fades, records get lost, and insurance companies become more aggressive in their defenses over time." },
  { question: "Do I need an attorney for a no-fault PIP claim?", answer: "While you can file a PIP claim on your own, having an attorney is highly recommended—especially if your insurance company denies benefits, terminates your care, or fails to pay claims promptly. Insurance companies have teams of lawyers working to minimize your benefits. You deserve experienced legal representation to level the playing field. Our attorneys handle all communications with insurance companies, challenge improper denials, and ensure you receive the full benefits you're owed—all on a contingency-fee basis." },
];
